QUESTION [c17ac41366c6]: How long does sunlight take to reach Earth, on average?

Seat weights from memory (before this run): atlas=1.45, vega=1.04, orion=1.35

  [atlas] answer A via openrouter:dots-studio/dots-3-note-preview:free (17.7s)  critic score: 9.0/10
     Sunlight takes about 8 minutes and 20 seconds to travel from the Sun to Earth, based on the average distance of 149.6 million km and the speed of light.
     sources: https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/sunfact.html, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunlight, https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_and_time/The_Sun
  [vega] answer B via openrouter:nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning:free (22.3s)  critic score: 10.0/10
     Sunlight takes approximately 8 minutes and 20 seconds to reach Earth on average. This is derived from the average Earth-Sun distance of about 149.6 million kilometers (1 astronomical unit) divided by the speed of light (~299,792 km/s). The time varies slightly due to Earth's elliptical orbit, ranging from about 490 seconds at perihelion to 510 seconds at aphelion.
     sources: https://earthsky.org/astronomy-essentials/how-long-does-sunlight-take-to-reach-earth/, https://nasa.gov/mission_pages/sunearth/spaceweather/sun/Earth.html, https://www.universetoday.com/118831/how-long-does-light-take-to-reach-earth/
  [orion] answer C via openrouter:recursive/flint-v1.0:free (11.6s)  critic score: 9.0/10
     The common answer is about 8 minutes. The contrarian tweak: the average is roughly 8 minutes 20 seconds, not a fixed 8:00. Because Earth orbits an ellipse, sunlight takes about 8 minutes 9 seconds at perihelion and 8 minutes 28 seconds at aphelion, averaging near 8:20. The old 8-minute figure is a rounded misconception that ignores orbital eccentricity. So average light travel time is about 499 seconds, though instan
     sources: https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/solar-system/earth/facts/, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_Earth_distance

