QUESTION [96636e38812e]: Did the Great Wall of China get seen with the naked eye from low Earth orbit?

Seat weights from memory (before this run): atlas=1.52, vega=1.20, orion=1.44

  [atlas] answer C via openrouter:dots-studio/dots-3-note-preview:free (29.0s)  critic score: 9.0/10
     No, the Great Wall of China cannot be seen with the naked eye from low Earth orbit. The wall is too narrow and blends with the surrounding terrain; from an altitude of about 400 km it is below the resolution limit of the human eye. Astronauts and NASA have confirmed that it is not visible without optical aid.
     sources: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Wall_of_China, https://www.space.com/26780-great-wall-of-china-visible-from-space.html, https://www.britannica.com/story/is-the-great-wall-of-china-visible-from-space
  [vega] answer A via openrouter:nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning:free (18.5s)  critic score: 10.0/10
     The claim that the Great Wall of China is visible to the naked eye from low Earth orbit is a persistent myth. The Wall is too narrow (averaging 5–8 m wide) and its materials blend with the surrounding terrain, making it indistinguishable to unaided eyes from orbit. NASA and astronaut confirmations state it is not visible without optical aid.
     sources: https://earthobservatory.nasa.gov/features/GreatWall, https://www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/station/news/visible-from-space.html
  [orion] answer B via openrouter:recursive/flint-v1.0:free (11.9s)  critic score: 8.0/10
     No—at least not reliably. The Great Wall is only about 5–7 meters wide and blends with terrain. From low Earth orbit, its angular size is near the eye’s resolution limit and contrast is poor. Most astronauts and NASA imagery indicate it isn’t discernible by naked eye from typical low Earth orbit. The common “visible from space” myth conflates telephoto photography with human vision. Under rare, ideal lighting or orbi
     sources: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Wall_of_China, https://www.britannica.com/topic/Great-Wall-of-China, https://www.history.com/topics/china/great-wall

