QUESTION [5d993db22999]: What causes the seasons on Earth?

Seat weights from memory (before this run): atlas=1.57, vega=1.31, orion=1.47

  [atlas] answer A via openrouter:dots-studio/dots-3-note-preview:free (31.7s)  critic score: 10.0/10
     The seasons are caused by Earth's axial tilt of about 23.5° relative to its orbital plane. As Earth orbits the Sun, the tilt causes different regions to receive varying amounts of direct sunlight and differing day lengths throughout the year, producing spring, summer, autumn, and winter.
     sources: https://earth.nasa.gov/seasons/, https://www.noaa.gov/education/resource/seasons, https://science.nasa.gov/planets/earth/seasons
  [vega] answer B via openrouter:nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning:free (10.3s)  critic score: 9.0/10
     Earth's seasons are caused by the 23.5° tilt of its rotational axis relative to its orbit around the Sun. As Earth revolves, this tilt means different hemispheres receive varying intensities and durations of sunlight at different times of the year, not because of distance from the Sun, but due to the angle of sunlight. When the Northern Hemisphere tilts toward the Sun, it experiences summer; when it tilts away, winte
     sources: https://earthobservatory.nasa.gov/features/Seasons, https://www.climate.gov/weather/seasonal-climate/what-causes-seasons, https://www.ucar.edu/learn/000-seasons
  [orion] answer C via openrouter:recursive/flint-v1.0:free (11.6s)  critic score: 8.0/10
     The common misconception is that Earth’s distance from the Sun causes seasons. It doesn’t, though orbit changes slightly. The primary cause is Earth’s axial tilt of about 23.4 degrees. As the tilted axis points toward or away from the Sun through the year, sunlight hits at varying angles and durations, driving seasonal changes. The hemispheres opposite each other because of this tilt. Distance is minor.
     sources: https://science.nasa.gov/earth/our-earth/seasons/, https://www.britannica.com/science/season

