QUESTION [061d84036ae9]: Who first proposed the theory of continental drift, and in what year?

Seat weights from memory (before this run): atlas=1.63, vega=1.38, orion=1.49

  [atlas] answer B via openrouter:dots-studio/dots-3-note-preview:free (14.5s)  critic score: 7.0/10
     Alfred Wegener, a German meteorologist and geophysicist, first proposed the theory of continental drift in 1912, later expanding it in his 1915 book The Origin of Continents and Oceans.
     sources: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continental_drift, https://www.britannica.com/science/continental-drift, https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-continental-drift
  [vega] answer C via openrouter:nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning:free (10.7s)  critic score: 7.0/10
     Alfred Wegener first proposed the theory of continental drift in 1912. A German meteorologist and geophysicist, he hypothesized that continents were once joined in a supercontinent called Pangaea and have since drifted apart, supporting his argument with fossil and geological evidence across continents.
     sources: https://www.britannica.com/science/continental-drift, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continental_drift, https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-continental-drift
  [orion] answer A via openrouter:recursive/flint-v1.0:free (12.1s)  critic score: 9.0/10
     The common attribution is Alfred Wegener in 1912, but the contrarian correction is that Wegener did not invent continental drift from nothing. Earlier geologists speculated about moving landmasses; Wegener then assembled the first detailed, evidence-based theory in 1912, publishing it in 1915. So the usual quick answer is Wegener, 1912/1915, but the misconception is treating him as the sole first proposer.
     sources: https://www.britannica.com/science/continental-drift, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continental_drift, https://www.nationalgeographic.com/science/article/continental-drift

